Accessing memory after it has been released can cause unpredictable behavior. The program might crash or corrupt data if that memory has already been reused. An allocation left unreleased wastes memory; in a long-running server, repeated leaks can build up until the server slows down or runs out of memory. A command-line program that exits after a second is less likely to suffer lasting harm from a leak because the operating system reclaims its memory on exit. But accessing released memory can still cause problems before the program exits
